course: money
lesson: 28
title: What a bank does with your money and why banks lend
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A bank does not just sit on your money: it lends money to other people and businesses, charges them more interest than it pays you, and keeps the difference.
explain: Picture Kai's £100: the bank pays him 2%, which is £2, and lends £100 to someone at 7%, who pays £7; the bank keeps £7 − £2 = £5, and that is how banks make money.
explain: Banks lend to help people buy homes and start businesses, like Ruby's aunt opening a bakery, but first they check the borrower can pay back, because a loan that is never repaid is lost money.
explain: Banks depend on trust: if everyone rushed to take their money out at once, there would not be enough cash in the building, so countries protect savers' money up to a limit and watch banks closely.
fact: Banks lend saved money to people and businesses and charge them interest; borrowers pay more interest than savers get.
fact: If a bank pays a saver £2 on £100 and earns £7 lending £100, it keeps £7 − £2 = £5 profit.
fact: A business loan helps a business start or grow; it is paid back bit by bit, and because of interest the total paid back is more than was borrowed, like £12,000 back on a £10,000 loan.
fact: If a bank lends £100 to each of 10 people and 3 cannot pay back, it loses 3 × £100 = £300.
fact: In 2008 some big banks had lent to people who could not pay back and nearly failed; governments stepped in with bailouts, and people still argue about whether that was right.
fact: Banks check whether a borrower is creditworthy, which means likely to pay back, by looking at income and past borrowing; uneven income makes a bank more careful.
fact: Credit cards charge high interest, like 20% compared with 3% on savings, because lending on cards is risky for banks.
fact: The central bank, like the Bank of England, sets the main interest rate, called the base rate; when it goes up, borrowing usually gets dearer and saving pays a bit more.
fact: Switching banks can earn more: 4% is £4 for every £100, so £40 on £1,000, while 1% is £10, so switching gives £30 more a year.
fact: A bank run is when lots of people take their money out at once; a bank could run out of cash, which is why trust matters so much.
fact: Online banks have no branches but keep money as records, like any bank, and in the UK savers in both get the same deposit protection.
exercise: What does a bank do with savers' money? | Lends it to others and charges them interest | Keeps every single note locked in a vault for ever | Gives it all away to local charities each Christmas | Burns the old notes and prints brand-new ones
exercise: The bank pays Kai £2 on his £100 and earns £7 lending £100 to someone else. How much does the bank keep? | £5 | £9 | £2 | £7
exercise: Ruby's aunt borrows £10,000 for a bakery and pays back £12,000 in all. How much interest is that? | £2,000 | £12,000 | £22,000 | £1,200
exercise: Why do banks lend to businesses like the bakery? | They earn interest, and the business can grow and create jobs | Banks are charities that must give money away | The government orders banks to fund every bakery | Banks want to own all the cakes in town
exercise: A bank lends £100 to each of 10 people and 3 can't pay back. How much does it lose? | £300 | £700 | £30 | £1,000
exercise: Grandad says the banks "went pear-shaped" in 2008. What happened? | Banks had lent to people who couldn't pay back, and some nearly failed | Banks ran out of paper to print bank statements | All the money in the world was stolen in one night | Banks stopped lending to anyone for ever
exercise: What is a bailout? | When a government helps a failing bank | When a bank throws out its old furniture | When a customer takes all their money out | When a bank refuses to lend to anyone
exercise: Kai's £100 is in the bank, but the bank has lent most of it out. Can Kai still take out £20? | Yes, the bank keeps enough cash for people taking out a bit | No, his money is gone until the borrower pays it back | Only if he waits for the borrower to come to the bank | No, savers can never take money out of a bank
exercise: Why won't the bank lend to Sophie's uncle right now? | His income isn't steady, so they worry he can't pay back | Banks never lend to people with uncles | His name is too long for the form | The bank has completely run out of money to lend for the year
exercise: Mia's savings earn 3% and Mum's credit card charges 20%. Why such a gap? | Lending on cards is risky, and banks earn from the difference | The bank likes children more than parents | Savings and cards are run by different countries with different rules | It is a mistake that the bank will fix soon
exercise: The central bank puts the base rate up. What usually happens to borrowing? | It gets dearer, so loans cost more interest | It gets cheaper, so everyone borrows more | It stops, because nobody may borrow any more | Nothing, because rates never touch loans
exercise: What is the central bank? | The country's main bank that sets the main interest rate | The bank in the middle of every town centre | The bank where the King keeps all of his own pocket money | The biggest online bank in the country
exercise: Dev's dad moves £1,000 from a bank paying 1% to one paying 4%. How much more interest does he get a year? | £30 | £40 | £3 | £50
exercise: Why do some banks have huge, grand buildings? | They want people to trust them as safe and solid | The law says every bank must be built to look like a castle | They keep each customer's notes in its own little room | Grand buildings earn the bank extra interest each year
exercise: What is a bank run? | When lots of people take their money out at once | A race the bank holds every year for its customers | A new branch opening its doors in a big shopping centre | When a bank lends money to runners for their trainers
exercise: Charlie's online bank has no building. Where is his money? | Kept as records in the bank's systems, like any bank | Nowhere, so it isn't real money and can't be spent anywhere | In a locked box hidden at the boss's house under the stairs | In Charlie's phone, and only there, so losing it loses all
test: Your mate goes, "Banks are mid, they just guard a big pile of cash and do nothing." ("Mid" means meh.) What's wrong? | Banks lend most of it out and earn interest on the loans | Nothing, banks really only guard cash | Banks spend it all on their own buildings | Banks give all of it to the government every Friday afternoon
test: A Geordie lad says, "Me uncle's work's dead up and down, so the bank says nah to his loan, howay man!" ("Howay man" means "come on!") Why did the bank say no? | With uneven income, they worry he can't pay it back | Banks don't lend to anyone at all from the North East | The bank thinks his name is too unusual to put on a form | Loans are only for people who have no job and need help
test: Leo says, "If the bank lent out my money, it's basically gone, that's well sus." What's the honest answer? | It isn't gone: his account still shows it, and he can take it out | He's right, lent money never comes back to anyone | Yes, and the bank will only ever give back half of it | No, because banks never lend savers' money to anybody
test: What would happen if everyone at one bank tried to take all their money out on the same morning? | The bank might not have enough cash to pay everyone at once | The bank would print more notes in the back room | Everyone would get double their money back | Nothing, because banks keep every saver's notes in separate drawers
test: Grandad, a bit salty about 2008, says, "The government should've let them banks sink." His neighbour says, "If they'd sunk, ordinary folk would've lost their savings." ("Salty" means bitter.) What's the fair summary? | People still argue about whether helping the banks was right | Grandad is right and every expert agrees | The neighbour is right and nobody disagrees | Nothing happened to banks in 2008 at all
test: Dev's dad says, "Loyalty doesn't pay, son." Dev asks, "Why not just stay?" On £1,000, the new bank pays 3% — sorry, 4% — and the old one 1%. How much more a year does he get by switching? | £30 | £20 | £40 | £10
test: In a cartoon, a bank lends to everyone without asking any questions, and the bank grows richer every day. What would really happen? | Some couldn't pay back, and the bank could lose lots of money | Everyone would always pay back on time, so it's fine | The bank would run out of loans but not money | Nothing, because unpaid loans cost banks nothing
test: Your aunt says, "Online banks are dodgy, no building, so where's the money?" What's true? | Money in any bank is kept as records; online banks have the same protection | She's right, only banks with buildings are real | Online banks keep money in a cloud in the sky | Online banks give money back only in cash
test: A Gen Z cousin says, "Base rate's going up, highkey bad for Mum's loan." ("Highkey" means very obviously.) Why is it bad for the loan? | Borrowing gets dearer, so Mum may pay more interest | The loan must be paid back in full the very next day | The bank will take Mum's car away the next morning | It isn't bad, because higher rates make loans free
test: Mia goes, "Why's the bank pay me 3% but charge Mum 20%? That's daylight robbery!" What's the best explanation? | Banks earn from the difference; card lending is risky | The bank is cheating her, and it's against the law to do it | Interest rates are picked each week by spinning a big wheel | Mum must have broken some bank rule last year without knowing
test: The bank pays 2% on £100 and lends it at 7%. If the borrower never pays back the £100, roughly how does the bank do? | It loses money, since £100 lent is gone and only small interest was earned | It still makes its £5 profit as normal | It makes even more, because unpaid loans earn double | It breaks even, because the saver pays for it
exam: It is given that a bank remunerates a depositor at two per cent and lends the same £100 at seven per cent. Determine the sum retained by the bank in one year. | £5 | £9 | £2 | £7
exam: Identify the institution which, in the United Kingdom, sets the principal rate of interest. | The Bank of England | The local council | The Royal Mint in Wales | The Post Office
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal source of a bank's profit? | The difference between the interest charged to borrowers and that paid to savers | The sale of the bank's buildings to the government every year | A fee levied upon each customer for entering a branch | The printing of banknotes for the bank's own use
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bank assesses the creditworthiness of an applicant before advancing a loan. | The protection of depositors' funds against loans that are not repaid | The compilation of a record of the applicant's hobbies | Compliance with a requirement that loans be refused to all applicants | The determination of the colour of the applicant's account
